---
/* Kyou's privacy policy. The App Store requires a link to this page from
   inside the app and from the listing, so the route is short and it never
   moves: petermei.com/kyou/privacy. The words are the ones in Kyou's own
   docs/privacy.md; keep the two in step whenever the app changes what it
   keeps. Plain prose on the site's paper, no games in the way of it. */
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';

const updated = 'September 8, 2026';
---

<Site
  title="Kyou Privacy Policy"
  desc="What Kyou does with your information: nothing leaves your phone, there is no account and no server, and your events and to-dos stay in Apple’s Calendar and Reminders."
  route="project"
  back="/kyou"
>
  <section class="legal" data-nav-tone="light" aria-label="Kyou privacy policy">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="h-title">Kyou Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="legal-date">Last updated {updated}</p>

      <p class="legal-lead">
        I’m Peter, and I made Kyou by myself. This page is everything the app does with your
        information.
      </p>

      <p class="legal-lead">Kyou collects nothing. There is no account, no sign-in, and no server of mine anywhere.</p>

      <p>
        The reason that holds is boring. There is no network code in the app: no requests, no web
        view, no outside libraries that could carry one in. Careful handling does not come into it.
        There is nowhere for your data to go.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some buttons take you out of Kyou. They go to its App Store listing, to iOS Settings, and to
        this page. Tell a friend hands the line “Kyou. Your whole day on one screen.” to whatever you
        share it with. Nothing about you goes along.
      </p>

      <h2>Calendar and Reminders</h2>
      <p>
        Kyou needs your calendar and your reminders. iOS asks you before it gets either. It reads the
        days it is drawing, plus the month sitting behind the date strip, so your events and to-dos
        can go on the timeline. It writes back when you add, edit, move, tick off or delete something
        yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of it lives in Kyou. It lives in Apple’s Calendar and Reminders on your phone, where it
        already was. Delete Kyou and every event and to-do it ever showed you is still sitting there,
        untouched.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can hide individual calendars and lists inside Kyou. That changes nothing in Apple’s apps;
        those items just stop appearing here. Either permission can be switched off in iOS Settings
        whenever you want.
      </p>

      <h2>What Kyou keeps on your phone</h2>
      <p>
        Your settings. Notification times, which calendars and lists you hid, and whether haptics are
        on.
      </p>
      <p>
        A copy of the next seven days, written into the app’s own container so the home screen and
        Lock Screen widgets have something to draw when the app isn’t running. Names, times, colors,
        and whether a thing is done.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the small bookkeeping: the color a tag was given, the wording of a notification that was
        already written, and the due date a repeating to-do was showing when you ticked it. Some of
        those hold the name of an item. All of it sits inside the app and goes when the app goes.
      </p>

      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        The app schedules its alerts on your phone. Nothing is pushed from anywhere, because there is
        nowhere to push from. An alert says the name of the thing it is about, so it turns up on your
        Lock Screen the way any notification does. Each kind can be turned off in Kyou’s settings, and
        all of them in iOS Settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Siri, Shortcuts, and Spotlight</h2>
      <p>
        Use one of Kyou’s shortcuts and the app hands the coming week to your iPhone’s Spotlight
        index: the day, and the names of the things on it. That is what makes searching for “chem lab”
        turn up the day it sits on. The index belongs to your phone and stays there, and Kyou clears
        out the days behind it as they go past.
      </p>
      <p>Whatever you say to Siri is between you and Apple. It arrives at Kyou as typed text and I never see it.</p>

      <h2>Apple Intelligence</h2>
      <p>
        On phones that have it, Apple’s model does two small jobs, both on the phone: reading the
        sentence you type into quick add, and wording a notification. The text goes nowhere and trains
        nothing. On phones without it, Kyou’s own parser reads the sentence and a set of written lines
        covers the notifications.
      </p>

      <h2>The tip jar</h2>
      <p>
        Kyou is free, all of it. If you leave a tip, the App Store handles the payment. Your name,
        address and card details never reach me. I learn that somebody tipped. That’s it.
      </p>

      <h2>What Kyou has never had</h2>
      <p>
        No ads. No analytics, no crash reporting service, no tracking, no advertising identifier, no
        outside code of any kind. It never asks for your location, photos, contacts or health data, so
        there is nothing there to switch off.
      </p>

      <h2>Two exceptions</h2>
      <p>
        Email me and I have your email address and whatever you wrote in it. It’s an ordinary email and
        I do nothing else with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Kyou crashes and you have iOS set to share crash reports with developers, Apple may show me
        a crash log. Apple decides what goes in one, and I read it in App Store Connect. The app sends
        me nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>If this changes</h2>
      <p>
        A version of Kyou that starts collecting something gets a new date at the top of this page and
        a line here saying what it collects and why. There is nothing to list yet.
      </p>
      <p>Questions go to the address on Kyou’s App Store listing.</p>

      <p class="legal-sign">Peter</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>

